# OpenConstructionERP — backend environment variables
# Copy this file to `backend/.env` and edit the values you need.
# Lines starting with `#` are comments; uncomment to override the default.

# ─── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Default is SQLite stored at `./openestimate.db` (works out of the box).
# To use PostgreSQL, UNCOMMENT both lines below and edit credentials:
#
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://user:password@localhost:5432/openestimate
# DATABASE_SYNC_URL=postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/openestimate

# ─── Auth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# JWT_SECRET MUST be a random 32+ character string in production.
# Generate with: `openssl rand -hex 32`
# (or `python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"`).
# The backend refuses to boot in staging/production with a value shorter
# than 32 chars or matching any well-known default ("change-me",
# "secret", "jwt-secret", "openestimate-local-dev-key", etc.).
# Left unset on purpose. The previous value here was the bundled dev key with
# a suffix, which is NOT on the denylist and is longer than the minimum, so it
# satisfied both guards and booted in production carrying a secret printed in
# a public repository. A placeholder that passes the check is worse than one
# that fails it: the one that fails refuses to start and says why.
# JWT_SECRET=

# ─── Vector search (AI Semantic Search for CWICR / Match Elements) ────
# Default backend is Qdrant. Install with:
#   pip install 'openconstructionerp[semantic]'   # qdrant-client
# To use the legacy LanceDB backend instead, install:
#   pip install 'openconstructionerp[vector]'     # lancedb
# and uncomment the line below.
# VECTOR_BACKEND=lancedb

# Qdrant server URL (only used when VECTOR_BACKEND=qdrant).
# QDRANT_URL=http://localhost:6333

# Fetch the semantic-search encoder weights in the background on startup.
# Default: on for a desktop or local install (OE_DESKTOP / a frozen build),
# off for a server deploy, which does not need them. Set it explicitly to
# override in either direction - "1" to fetch them on a server, "0" to keep a
# workstation from ever downloading them.
#
# The download is a non-critical extra in the strict sense: startup never
# waits for it, a failure or an interruption leaves the application exactly as
# usable as it was, and semantic search says out loud that it is unavailable
# while lexical search keeps answering. It needs the [semantic] extra to be
# installed - without it there is nothing that could load the weights, and the
# installer refuses rather than fetching hundreds of megabytes for nothing.
#
# Weights land under <data dir>/models/. Set HUGGINGFACE_CACHE_DIR to put them
# somewhere else on a host where that path is not writable.
# OE_DOWNLOAD_EMBEDDING_MODEL=1
# Where the weights are fetched from. Point this at a mirror when huggingface.co
# is unreachable, which is the usual case behind a corporate proxy or in an
# air-gapped network. Wrong value costs you the download and nothing else: the
# installer fails, says so, and the application stays exactly as usable.
# HF_ENDPOINT=https://huggingface.co

# ─── Optional services ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0
# S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
# S3_ACCESS_KEY=minioadmin
# S3_SECRET_KEY=minioadmin

# ─── AI providers (optional — required for AI Chat / Cost Advisor) ────
# Every provider key the app looks for in the environment. A key set in
# Settings > AI takes precedence; these are the fallback for headless and
# container deployments where nobody opens the settings page.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...
# MISTRAL_API_KEY=
# GROQ_API_KEY=gsk_...
# DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=
# Self-hosted AI provider endpoints (Ollama / vLLM) are fetched server-side.
# Loopback and private addresses are allowed by default so a local runtime works
# out of the box, while link-local and cloud-metadata addresses are always
# blocked. Set a comma-separated allowlist of hostnames and/or CIDR ranges to
# restrict AI provider URLs to a known set.
# OE_AI_PROVIDER_ALLOWLIST=ollama.internal,10.20.0.0/16
# Where those two runtimes actually live. Defaults assume both run on this host,
# so a runtime on another machine is unreachable until you say where it is.
# OE_OLLAMA_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1/chat/completions
# OE_OLLAMA_MODEL=llama3.1
# OE_VLLM_URL=http://localhost:8001/v1/chat/completions
# OE_VLLM_MODEL=meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct
# "vllm" is the generic OpenAI-compatible endpoint: vLLM, an Ollama-style
# server, or a gateway in front of either. The URL may be the server root, end
# in /v1, or be the full /v1/chat/completions path. When the server checks a
# bearer key, set it here or in Settings > AI (the saved key wins).
# OE_VLLM_API_KEY=
# Tool calling for self-hosted endpoints is off unless told otherwise, because
# many local servers reject the tools field. Providers listed here start in
# "auto": the app sends tools, and when the server refuses them it retries
# without and remembers that endpoint and model for an hour. Settings > AI can
# set auto, on or off per endpoint.
# OE_AI_TOOLS_SELF_HOSTED=vllm,ollama
# Seconds an AI request may run before it is cut, clamped to 10..1800.
# OE_AI_TIMEOUT covers every AI call (default 240). OE_CHAT_AI_TIMEOUT covers
# the AI chat only (default 120 for Anthropic and OpenAI, OE_AI_TIMEOUT for the
# rest). A timeout saved per provider in Settings > AI wins over both. The web
# app still gives up on requests outside the chat after 5 minutes, and the
# split deployment's nginx cuts /api/ at 120 s (proxy_read_timeout), so raise
# that too if you set a longer budget there.
# OE_AI_TIMEOUT=600
# OE_CHAT_AI_TIMEOUT=600

# ─── ERP chat budget ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Tokens the chat may spend per day across the whole install. Raising it raises
# what a busy day can cost you at the provider; lowering it means the chat stops
# answering once the budget is gone.
# ERPCHAT_DAILY_TOKEN_BUDGET=500000

# ─── Rate limiting (requests per minute) ──────────────────────────────
# API_RATE_LIMIT=100
# LOGIN_RATE_LIMIT=10
# AI_RATE_LIMIT=10

# ─── PDF takeoff limits ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# These bound what one uploaded PDF can do to the host. The defaults are sized
# for a small box; raise them if real drawings are being refused, and know that
# every one of them is the ceiling that keeps a hostile or pathological file
# from taking the process down with it.
# OE_TAKEOFF_MAX_UPLOAD_MB=200        # bigger uploads accepted, more RAM at risk
# OE_TAKEOFF_MAX_PAGES=300            # pages parsed per file, clamped 1-5000
# OE_TAKEOFF_PARSE_TIMEOUT_S=120      # parser killed after this, clamped 10-1800
# OE_TAKEOFF_PARSE_MEM_MB=1536        # parser address-space cap, clamped 256-8192
# OCR of scanned sheets. Higher DPI reads small text better and costs time and
# memory on every page; the language list decides which scripts are recognised
# at all, so a sheet in a script you left out simply will not be read.
# OE_TAKEOFF_OCR_DPI=200              # clamped 72-600
# OE_TAKEOFF_OCR_LANGS=en,hi,ta,te,ar

# ─── DWG conversion ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Wall-clock budget for one drawing conversion, and how often a running job
# reports that it is still alive. Raise the timeout for large drawings on slow
# hardware; too low and a legitimate conversion is killed halfway.
# OE_DWG_CONVERT_TIMEOUT_S=300
# OE_DWG_HEARTBEAT_S=30

# ─── Pipeline execution caps ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Ceilings on one pipeline run. They exist so a runaway or cyclic graph cannot
# occupy a worker indefinitely; raising them raises how long one bad run holds.
# PIPELINE_MAX_NODES=256
# PIPELINE_NODE_TIMEOUT_S=300

# ─── Geocoding (address lookup) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Address lookup goes to public OpenStreetMap services by default, so every
# lookup leaves this host. Point them at your own instances to keep addresses
# in-house, or switch the lookups off entirely and keep manual coordinates.
# Both switches read 1, true, yes or on as off; anything else leaves lookup on.
# OE_GEOCODER_DISABLED=0              # on: no address lookup at all
# OE_GEOCODER_BASE_URL=https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org
# OE_GEOCODER_PHOTON_DISABLED=0       # on: drops the Photon fallback only
# OE_GEOCODER_PHOTON_URL=https://photon.komoot.io
# Nominatim's policy wants a contact in the User-Agent. One is already sent, and
# it is ours, so an unconfigured deploy is compliant but any rate-limit notice
# about your traffic reaches us rather than you. Setting this replaces it.
# OE_GEOCODER_USER_AGENT_CONTACT=ops@example.com

# ─── Demo seed ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set to false in production to skip seeding demo projects + accounts.
# SEED_DEMO=true
#
# The destructive admin endpoints next to this (demo reset, cost-vector
# reindex) are gated by env vars that are deliberately not listed in this file
# or anywhere else. They wipe or rebuild data, and a name in an example file is
# an invitation to try it. Anyone who genuinely needs them can read
# app/modules/admin/service.py, which is the intended bar.

# ─── Public read-only demo ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only for a demo instance that strangers can reach. On, every request that
# would change persistent state is refused with 403 and
# {"error": "demo_read_only", ...}; reads, and signing in, keep working. That
# way every visitor sees the seeded example projects rather than whatever the
# previous visitor typed. Leave it unset on a real install - the default is
# off, and turning it on makes the deployment read-only for its own operators
# too. The hosted demo also sets OE_DEMO_MODE, which is a different switch
# (analytics and redaction); it does not refuse anything on its own.
# OE_DEMO_READ_ONLY=false

# ─── Demo account passwords (auto-generated if unset, persisted to
#     ~/.openestimator/.demo_credentials.json — set explicitly here to
#     keep the documented canonical demo creds working out of the box).
# DEMO_USER_PASSWORD=DemoPass1234!
# DEMO_ESTIMATOR_PASSWORD=DemoPass1234!
# DEMO_MANAGER_PASSWORD=DemoPass1234!

# ─── Email / SMTP ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Outbound mail for password resets, tender invitations, document delivery
# and notifications. Entirely optional: left as it is, the platform runs
# normally and writes messages it would have sent to the application log.
#
# EMAIL_BACKEND is the setting people miss. Filling in the SMTP_* values
# below does nothing on its own - the default "console" transport records
# each message and reports it as delivered, so mail silently never leaves.
# Set EMAIL_BACKEND=smtp to actually send.
#
# Use port 587 with SMTP_TLS=true. Port 465 expects encryption from the
# first byte, which this transport does not speak, and is refused.
# The password setting is SMTP_PASSWORD, not SMTP_PASS.
# Full guide, including how to verify delivery: docs/email-setup.md
# EMAIL_BACKEND=console               # console | smtp | noop | memory
# SMTP_HOST=
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_TLS=true
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# SMTP_FROM=noreply@yourcompany.example
